  <static seed="424" source="csafranski/5.5aHW_prob1.pg">
    <statement><p>Which of the following is the correct form of the partial fraction decomposition of <m>\displaystyle{\frac{x-1}{x^3+x^2}}</m>? You may select more than one answer.</p>
<p><ol form="checkboxes" marker="A" name="AnSwEr0001">
<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle \frac{A}{x}+\frac{B}{x^2}+\frac{C}{x+1}</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle \frac{Ax+B}{x}+\frac{Cx+D}{x^2}+\frac{E}{x+1}</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle \frac{Ax+B}{x}+\frac{Cx+D}{x^2}+\frac{Ex+F}{x+1}</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle \frac{Ax+B}{x}+\frac{Cx+D}{x^2}+\frac{Ex+F}{x+1}</m></p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{A}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>The denominator factors as <me>x^3 + x^2 = x^2 (x+1).</me> 
Since the factored form of the denominator has the linear term <m>x</m> raised to the second power and the linear term <m>x+1</m>, the correct form  of the partial fraction is: 
<me>\frac{A}{x}+\frac{B}{x^2}+\frac{C}{x+1}</me></p>
</solution>
